title The record its own pass writes at the close
prompt Autonomous lap under the fleet baton -- BAKERY seat, core infrastructure, Lindy-first and crux-first.
think Lap 4067, so the rota reads row 2 -- fire, the row that sees. Its lesson picked the work: look hard at the fault most laps route around, then cut once, at exactly the depth the truth requires.
think Of six open reds, %483 was the crux and it was mine. The runner and the roster are the harness every one of eight ships stands on, and the row's own last line handed the ruling to that seat.
think The row named two shapes. Reading the runner showed the second one names a position that does not exist -- the card is written once, at the close, so nothing runs after the write. That leaves the first shape load-bearing and the second as its completion, which is why both landed rather than one.
think The obvious form of the first shape collides with a standing guarantee: writing the working-tree card incrementally moves the tree under the runner's own tree_moved reading, in every control pen and in any clone where the card is not yet gitignored. The pen is not the tree, so the live view lives there and reaches each guard through STANDING_CARD.
think That hand-off only works if rishi run passes the environment through. I proved that on metal with a three-line probe before writing a line of the repair, rather than assuming it.
obs THE FIRST HALF ALONE LEFT A RESIDUE, AND I COUNTED IT RATHER THAN WAVING AT IT. standing_equipment stands at roster position 188 of 196; eight guards follow it, seven of them tier lap. So the runner defers its own guard by NAME to the end of the todo list -- by name rather than by roster position, since a hand adding a guard alphabetically after it would silently reopen the gap.
obs THE FAILING SIDE DID NOT FAIL, AND THAT WAS THE PEN TELLING THE TRUTH. blind_runner_reads_stale read no on the first run. The blind copy sources shell_portable.sh from its OWN directory, so a copy standing anywhere else cannot start at all -- a refusal that looks exactly like the repair working. The sibling travels with the copy now, and a ninth reading asserts the blind runner actually reached a guard, since a copy that cannot run answers no for a reason having nothing to do with the hand-off.
obs THE LIVE TREE DEMONSTRATED %483 WHILE I WAS CLOSING IT. The cold open reddened on sow_allow_reach; I projected the seed and the guard went green on metal; the witness still refused, because the card still recorded the red. That is the row's own fault, standing in front of me, and the runner's by-name mode is what cleared it.
obs AND THAT RED WAS A SECOND, LARGER FINDING. sow_allow_reach joined the roster tier lap an hour before my round-open pulled it. Its precondition is a build artifact that cost 3m49s here, and six of eight fleet trees carry no seed/ at all -- so each reds every lap, and by %483's own mechanism each pays a full pass for it. Booked %492; the repair on peer trees is theirs by %291, and the capability field is a ruling for the guard's seat.
obs A GRADE BELOW B CLOSED WITH A MEASUREMENT RATHER THAN A REWRITE. The witness reads C+/77 -- and read exactly C+/77 before this lap, 936 words and 39 sentences both ways. The cause is the standing Door-versus-Meter question (%417, gate %7) over a file whose prose is refusal messages. What a depth-2 frame would have pushed became a card line instead: qa_report_card.sh reads a program's LEADING comment block alone.
obs THE REBASE TAUGHT MORE THAN THE LAP DID. Upstream had moved five commits, and a peer had booked my finding nine minutes earlier as %493. I withdrew my %492 whole and moved its three measurements into their row as a clause -- one defect, one row, the earlier stamp keeping the number, which is what the derived-spine law asks for.
obs AND THE CARD ARRIVED WITH TWO DEAD LINKS. Walking its own 36 relative links with [ -f ] found 2 resolving nowhere; readme_reach then named both by path. The root is one rename: 4cc3eb0d0 created a shelf at rows-492 and linked it correctly, b441f97b2 renamed it to rows-494 without repointing, and spent the freed number on the neighbouring block's link. References are promises, and a rename is where one breaks quietly. Booked %495, both repointed against each shelf's own heading rather than its filename.
obs AND THE SECOND WITHDRAWAL CAME ON THE NEXT REBASE. My whole broken-link row was already booked as %495 by the hand that made the mistake, from the inside and with the truer root: their rename sweep called replace on a bare rows-<number>.md, so the first match in the card was a peer's line rather than their own. I inferred a rename without a repoint; they measured a sweep without an anchor. Withdrawn whole -- two withdrawals in one lap, to two peers, both within the hour.
obs THE GUARD WAS NEVER MISSING, WHICH IS THE HALF I ALMOST GOT WRONG. My first draft of %495 said nothing on the roster reads living links. readme_reach is tier lap and holds living_files_affected at zero -- it caught both on the first pass after the rebase. The honest finding is a send that published past a zero-gate, twice, and how is unmeasured from here.
